      heading: "Does shockwave therapy actually work for ED?",
      body: `<p><strong>The honest answer: for some men it produces a real but modest and often temporary improvement - and for others it does little. The evidence shows a small average benefit, concentrated in men with mild-to-moderate vascular ED, but the quality of that evidence is low, and it is not a reliable cure.</strong> Anyone who tells you it "works" (or "doesn't work") as a flat yes-or-no is oversimplifying.</p><p>Here's the nuanced truth the research supports:</p><ul><li>Pooled analyses of randomized trials <em>do</em> show a statistically significant average improvement in erectile-function scores after Li-ESWT, measured at 1, 3 and 6 months.</li><li>But the <em>size</em> of that average improvement is small, the studies are mostly small and inconsistent, and the overall certainty of the evidence is rated <strong>low</strong> by systematic reviewers.</li><li>The men most likely to benefit have <strong>mild-to-moderate, blood-flow-related (vascular) ED</strong> - not severe ED, not nerve-damage ED, not psychological ED.</li><li>Even in responders, the benefit often <strong>fades over months to a couple of years</strong>, which is why clinics sell maintenance sessions.</li></ul><p>So "does it work?" is really "works how much, for whom, and for how long?" - and the honest answers are "modestly," "a specific subgroup," and "often not permanently." That's a genuine treatment with a genuine (if limited) signal - not a miracle, and not a scam, but heavily oversold. The rest of this guide unpacks each of those qualifiers.</p>`,
