      body: `<p><strong>The theory is that low-intensity acoustic waves create tiny, controlled stresses in penile tissue that trigger the body to grow new blood vessels (angiogenesis) and repair existing ones - improving the blood flow an erection depends on.</strong> It's worth stressing the word <em>theory</em>: this is the proposed mechanism, not a fully proven one.</p><p>The biological hypotheses, drawn from lab and early clinical work, are roughly:</p><ul><li><strong>Neovascularization / angiogenesis.</strong> The micro-stresses are thought to stimulate release of growth factors (like VEGF) that prompt new blood-vessel formation and improve the health of the vessel lining (endothelium).</li><li><strong>Recruitment of repair cells.</strong> Shockwaves may attract stem/progenitor cells to the treated tissue, supporting regeneration.</li><li><strong>Possible nerve effects.</strong> Some researchers propose minor benefits to nerve tissue, though this is even less established.</li></ul><p>If that mechanism were robustly proven and durable, shockwave would be genuinely exciting - because unlike a pill, it would be treating a <em>cause</em> (poor blood flow) rather than producing a one-off response. That's exactly the hook the marketing leans on. The honest caveat: the mechanism is biologically plausible and supported by some lab data, but "plausible and partly supported" is not the same as "proven to regrow your blood vessels and fix your ED for good." The clinical results (small, variable, often temporary) are more modest than the regeneration story implies.</p>`,
