      heading: "Why shockwave is pitched so differently from Viagra",
      body: `<p><strong>Pills and shockwave are sold on opposite promises: a PDE5 inhibitor like Viagra openly treats the symptom on demand, while shockwave is marketed as treating the underlying cause so you won't need pills - a "fix it" story versus a "manage it" story.</strong> Understanding this framing is key to seeing through the hype.</p><p>A PDE5 inhibitor (sildenafil, tadalafil) amplifies the blood-flow response when you're aroused. It works for the large majority of men, costs very little as a generic, and is the proven first-line treatment - but you take it each time, and it doesn't change your underlying vascular health. The honest knock on pills is simply that they're a recurring aid, not a cure.</p><p>Shockwave's entire marketing advantage is positioning against that: "stop renting erections, own them again." The promise of a durable, root-cause fix is powerful, especially for younger men who dislike the idea of relying on medication. But here's the reframe you need: the evidence that shockwave delivers on the "cure" promise is weak, while the evidence that pills deliver on the "reliable help" promise is overwhelming. One treatment makes a bigger promise with smaller proof; the other makes a smaller promise with enormous proof. Keep that asymmetry in mind for everything that follows. If you want to understand the proven option it's being sold against, our <a href="/articles/sildenafil-vs-cialis">sildenafil vs tadalafil</a> guide covers it.</p>`,
